      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. If a thicker end-grain chisel is the better option to prevent buckling, I'll look into sourcing one with a more robust profile. \n\nYou mentioned the \"springy\" nature of the end grain might still cause deflection even with more mass. Is there a specific way to gauge that springiness or resistance before I commit to a cut? I'm also wondering if the moisture content of the walnut plays into this \"springy\" behavior—if I were to slightly increase the moisture to make the fibers less brittle, would that actually help dampen the vibration, or would it just lead to more crushing and irregular fiber-lifting?",
      "source": "evaluator"
